var BANK_PARTS = BANK_PARTS || [];
BANK_PARTS.push([
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":3,"s":"A poll of people who wrote letters opposing a new harbor ordinance found that most thought the ordinance was confusing. The sponsor concludes that most city residents find the ordinance confusing.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the ordinance caused the confusion merely because the letters mention both","treats people who wrote opposing letters as though they stood for city residents generally","dismisses the letters because the writers might gain if the ordinance fails","slides from one meaning of \"confusing\" in the letters to another meaning in the conclusion","treats two proposed wordings of the ordinance as the only possible wordings"],"a":1,"e":"The letters come from opponents, and the conclusion is about residents at large. That group is being asked to stand in for a wider one. The attractive choice — infers that the ordinance caused the confusion merely because the letters mention both — loses because the conclusion is about whose opinions were counted, not about the ordinance producing confusion. A causal story is a different criticism from the narrow sample."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":3,"s":"The hospital board argues: either visiting hours are cut in half, or patients will get no rest. Visiting hours will not be cut in half. Therefore, patients will get no rest.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the whole hospital lacks rest because one ward lacks rest","dismisses a nurse's objection because of the nurse's schedule preferences","treats last month's restful nights as proof about next month","slides between a medical sense of \"rest\" and a legal sense of \"rest\"","treats a cut in visiting hours and a total loss of rest as the only two possibilities"],"a":4,"e":"From the refusal to cut hours, the board infers no rest at all. That follows only if those are the only options, and nothing has shown that they are. The attractive choice — infers that the whole hospital lacks rest because one ward lacks rest — loses because the argument does not move from a ward to the hospital. It pretends that cutting hours and losing all rest exhaust the ways rest might be protected."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"A celebrated novelist says the city should repeal its noise ordinance. Therefore, the city should repeal the noise ordinance.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["rejects the ordinance because of the motives of the council that passed it","infers repeal from a correlation between novel sales and noise complaints","treats a novelist's endorsement as settling a legal question outside the novelist's demonstrated field","generalizes from one block's noise to every block in the city, though the conclusion is citywide only in the novelist's mouth","treats quieter nights and repealed rules as the only two options"],"a":2,"e":"A legal repeal is inferred from a literary celebrity's remark, with no showing that the remark applies a relevant standard. The attractive choice — rejects the ordinance because of the motives of the council that passed it — loses because nobody's motive is attacked. The entire reason given is the novelist's say-so."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"No complaint log at the clinic mentions a missed allergy check. The director concludes that no allergy check has been missed.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["treats the lack of logged complaints as proof that no check was missed","takes for granted that \"missed\" in the logs means something other than \"missed\" in the conclusion","infers that the whole clinic missed checks because one department did","dismisses the nurses' concern because of the nurses' motives","draws the conclusion from a sample of patients who already had allergies"],"a":0,"e":"A log that never mentions a miss is being asked to prove there was no miss. Silence is not that proof unless a miss would have been logged. The attractive choice — takes for granted that \"missed\" in the logs means something other than \"missed\" in the conclusion — loses because the argument does not switch senses of a word. It promotes silence in the log into a clean bill of health."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Districts that adopted evening court hours saw civil filings rise in the same year. A commentator concludes that the evening hours caused the rise in filings.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["takes for granted that civil filings cannot rise in a district that keeps only daytime hours","rejects the commentator because bar groups might gain from evening hours","treats the filings of districts that adopted the hours as if the conclusion were about districts that did not","concludes that the evening hours produced the rise solely because the hours and the rise occurred in the same year","uses \"filing\" for a criminal charge in the premise and a civil complaint in the conclusion"],"a":3,"e":"Same-year movement is the only link, and it is asked to prove that the hours caused the filings. The commentator does not invoke, or depend on, a rule that filings had to rise first. The attractive choice — takes for granted that civil filings cannot rise in a district that keeps only daytime hours — loses because the commentator does not say filings are impossible without evening hours. The commentator says the evening hours caused the rise in the districts that adopted them. A necessary-condition claim about every district is more than the argument uses."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Equivocation","d":3,"s":"The baker asked for a \"light\" loaf, meaning a loaf that is easy to carry. The lab sheet uses \"light\" for a loaf with little sugar. This loaf is light on the lab sheet, so a clerk says it is what the baker asked for.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["appeals to the lab as an authority on what bakers want to carry","slides from \"light\" as easy to carry to \"light\" as low in sugar","infers that low sugar caused the loaf to be easy to carry from the mere use of one word","generalizes from this loaf to every loaf the bakery sells","treats two recipes as the only recipes the baker will accept"],"a":1,"e":"The baker's \"light\" and the lab's \"light\" are different properties. The clerk's conclusion needs them to be one property. The attractive choice — appeals to the lab as an authority on what bakers want to carry — loses because the clerk does not treat the lab as an expert on carrying. The clerk treats a low-sugar reading as if it meant easy to carry."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":3,"s":"The mileage log is correct because it matches the driver's memory, and the driver's memory is correct because it matches the mileage log.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the driver's memory caused the log entries merely because the two agree","concludes that every company log is correct from this one log","treats the driver as an authority on odometers because the driver is well known","reads a drop in the percentage of checked trips as a drop in their number","offers each of two claims as the sole support for the other"],"a":4,"e":"Memory is vouched for by the log, and the log is vouched for by memory. Nothing outside the pair is offered. The attractive choice — infers that the driver's memory caused the log entries merely because the two agree — loses because the argument is not a causal claim about how the entries were produced. It is a closed loop of support."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":2,"s":"This kiln has reached the glaze temperature on every firing this month. The potter concludes that it will reach that temperature on the next firing.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the glaze temperature caused the pots to sell, because sales and firings are both part of the shop","treats this kiln as representative of every kiln in the guild","extends an unbroken record one firing forward without showing that the next firing matches the ones already run","appeals to a famous potter's praise of the kiln","lets the kiln and the pyrometer certify each other"],"a":2,"e":"A month of hits supports the next firing only if the next firing is like those hits. The argument supplies the streak and skips the comparison. The attractive choice — infers that the glaze temperature caused the pots to sell, because sales and firings are both part of the shop — loses because sales are not in the argument. The conclusion is about the next firing's temperature, not about a cause of sales."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"A quiet-car rule reduced complaints on the commuter rail. The transit chief concludes that the same rule will reduce complaints on the overnight ferry.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["transfers the rail result to the ferry without a likeness that matters to the complaints","infers that the quiet-car rule caused the rail drop merely from the rule and the drop being mentioned together, and the conclusion is only about the ferry","treats each rail car as quiet and therefore the whole train as quiet, which is not the conclusion","appeals to a celebrity rider's endorsement of quiet cars","treats the lack of ferry studies as proof the rule will fail, which is not what the chief concludes"],"a":0,"e":"Complaints fell under a rail rule, and that drop is handed to an overnight ferry. No shared cause of the complaints is supplied. The attractive choice — infers that the quiet-car rule caused the rail drop merely from the rule and the drop being mentioned together, and the conclusion is only about the ferry — loses because even if the rail premise were thinly causal, the argument's new step is the move to the ferry. The choice attacks a premise the argument treats as given and misses the analogy."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":2,"s":"The cellar floor was wet at dawn. The keeper concludes that the sump pump was left off overnight.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the pump caused the wet floor from a controlled comparison of two cellars","dismisses the keeper because the keeper sells pumps","treats the lack of a maintenance log as proof the pump is new","treats one possible cause of a wet floor as though it were the only available cause","gives the whole building a leak because the cellar is wet"],"a":3,"e":"A wet floor is compatible with a burst pipe, rain through a hatch, or a pump left off. The keeper picks the pump without closing the others. The attractive choice — infers that the pump caused the wet floor from a controlled comparison of two cellars — loses because there is no comparison of two cellars. There is one wet floor and a single favored cause. The flaw is the ignored alternatives, not a botched experiment."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":3,"s":"Volunteers who maintain the east creek path mostly said the path needs more gravel. The office concludes that most people who use the path want more gravel.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["A path ought to receive more gravel only if most of its users request gravel.","With respect to wanting more gravel, the maintainers' views match the views of path users.","Gravel costs less than a wooden boardwalk.","Most users live within a mile of the creek.","The volunteers were asked on a rainy afternoon."],"a":1,"e":"Maintainers were asked and users were concluded about. Unless those groups match on this question, the step fails. The attractive choice — A path ought to receive more gravel only if most of its users request gravel. — loses because that is a policy about when gravel is deserved. The argument does not conclude that the path ought to get gravel. It concludes that users want gravel. The policy can be false and the transfer can still be required."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":3,"s":"No inspector has found a split in the red kayak. The outfitter concludes that the red kayak has no split.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The outfitter has never missed a split in any kayak the inspectors did not inspect.","The red kayak is rented more often than the yellow kayak.","Inspectors look only at red kayaks.","A kayak can have at most one split.","If the red kayak had a split, an inspector would have found it."],"a":4,"e":"Not finding a split shows there is no split only if a split would have been found. Negate that link and the silence is uninformative. The attractive choice — The outfitter has never missed a split in any kayak the inspectors did not inspect. — loses because a perfect record on kayaks that were not inspected is not required, and it is barely coherent as support. What the conclusion needs is that a split in this kayak would have turned up. Other kayaks can be a muddle."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"A clipboard rule reduced lost badges at the craft museum. The director concludes that the same rule will reduce lost badges at the science hall.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Any rule that reduces losses at a small museum will reduce losses at every larger museum.","The science hall has more visitors per day.","Badges are lost for the same kind of reason, in a comparable handoff, at both museums.","The craft museum began the rule during a holiday week.","The science hall already uses numbered lockers for coats."],"a":2,"e":"Without a relevant likeness, the craft-museum result does not reach the science hall. The conclusion depends on that likeness. The attractive choice — Any rule that reduces losses at a small museum will reduce losses at every larger museum. — loses because the argument is about this clipboard and these two sites, not about every rule and every larger museum. That broader transfer can fail and this one can still be supported by a likeness in how badges go missing."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":2,"s":"Anyone who finishes the harbor radio course receives a yellow tag. Quinn has a yellow tag. Therefore, Quinn finished the harbor radio course.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The only way to receive a yellow tag is to finish the harbor radio course.","Everyone who finishes the harbor radio course receives a yellow tag.","The course is offered only on weekdays.","Most people who finish the course clip the tag to a collar.","The course was shortened last year."],"a":0,"e":"If inspectors, guests, or replacements also get yellow tags, Quinn's tag does not show Quinn finished. The conclusion depends on finishing being the only route. The attractive choice — Everyone who finishes the harbor radio course receives a yellow tag. — loses because that is the premise already stated, read back as if it closed the gap. The argument already has \"finish, then tag.\" What it needs, and does not have, is that there is no other route to Quinn's tag."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Equivocation","d":2,"s":"The gardener called the soil rich. The nursery uses \"rich\" for soil that needs no added fertilizer. A clerk concludes that this soil needs no added fertilizer.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["By \"rich,\" the gardener meant only that the soil looks dark.","The nursery sells fertilizer beside the soil bins.","In ordinary gardening talk, dark soil can still need fertilizer.","By \"rich,\" the gardener meant soil that needs no added fertilizer.","The gardener spoke in a different month from the clerk's conclusion."],"a":3,"e":"If the gardener's \"rich\" is not the nursery's \"rich,\" the conclusion does not follow. The clerk depends on those senses matching. The attractive choice — By \"rich,\" the gardener meant only that the soil looks dark. — loses because the dark-soil reading is a rival meaning. If it is true, the clerk's conclusion does not follow. A required assumption is something the argument needs, not a reading that destroys it. The needed claim is that the gardener's sense matches the nursery's."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"At the reef museum, postcards dropped from 20 percent of items sold to 10 percent. The buyer concludes that fewer postcards were sold.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The later period included a holiday weekend and the earlier period did not.","The shop did not sell enough additional other items to offset postcards' smaller share.","Postcards take more shelf space than pins.","The shop added a line of stuffed fish.","Some visitors bought more than one postcard."],"a":1,"e":"Negate the total constraint and postcard counts can rise while the share falls. That negation kills the conclusion, so the constraint is required. The attractive choice — The later period included a holiday weekend and the earlier period did not. — loses because a holiday can sit in either period without settling the count. The buyer does not need the calendar to have changed or to have stayed. The buyer needs the total not to have swallowed the smaller share."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":3,"s":"Requiring a signed clipboard reduced lost tools at the pottery studio. The owner concludes that the same rule will reduce lost tools at the boathouse.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The boathouse is larger than the studio, so a rule that works in a studio will fail in a boathouse.","The boathouse is closer to the water than the studio is.","The studio started the rule in July.","Clipboard ink fades in direct sun.","Tools go missing at the boathouse for the same sort of reason, and at a similar handoff, as at the studio."],"a":4,"e":"If the boathouse loses tools for a different reason, the clipboard result does not transfer. The conclusion depends on the losses matching. The attractive choice — The boathouse is larger than the studio, so a rule that works in a studio will fail in a boathouse. — loses because that predicts the transfer will fail. The owner's conclusion needs the opposite relationship: a likeness that lets the studio result carry. A size difference that sinks the analogy is not something the argument must assume."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":2,"s":"Any cabin rented in January is winterized. Cabin 4 is winterized. Therefore, cabin 4 is rented in January.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Cabin 4 is the only winterized cabin on the lake.","Cabin 4 has a wood stove as well as insulation.","Every winterized cabin is rented in January.","January rentals are inspected in December.","The lake freezes in January."],"a":2,"e":"Winterizing is necessary for a January rental in the premise. The universal claim that every winterized cabin is rented in January turns that necessity into a sufficient condition and yields the conclusion. The attractive choice — Cabin 4 is the only winterized cabin on the lake. — loses because uniqueness does not create a January rental. A single winterized cabin can sit empty all winter. The missing link is from being winterized to being rented, not from being the only winterized cabin."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Bikes fell from 40 percent of the vehicles in the garage to 25 percent. Therefore, the garage holds fewer bikes.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["The garage holds no more vehicles now than it held when bikes were 40 percent.","Twenty-five percent is a smaller share than 40 percent.","Bikes take less floor space than cars.","The garage was repainted between the two counts.","Some bikes are stored on wall racks."],"a":0,"e":"If the number of vehicles did not rise, a drop from 40 percent to 25 percent is a drop in the number of bikes. That premise is sufficient for the count. The attractive choice — Twenty-five percent is a smaller share than 40 percent. — loses because the smaller share is already the premise. Repeating it does not supply the missing total. A total that did not grow, added to the smaller share, is what forces the number of bikes down."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":2,"s":"The bridge carried each of the last ten harvest floats. Therefore, it will carry this year's float.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["This year's float uses the same fabric flowers as last year's float.","The parade is popular in town.","The bridge is made of cedar.","If the bridge carried each of the last ten harvest floats, then it will carry this year's float.","Last year's float arrived on time."],"a":3,"e":"The ten crossings are the antecedent of the conditional. Adding that conditional makes the conclusion follow at once. The attractive choice — This year's float uses the same fabric flowers as last year's float. — loses because matching decorations do not connect ten successful crossings to this crossing. The conditional does: the ten crossings are given, and the conditional yields this year."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":3,"s":"Okada says the bridge should close. Okada's cousin gains boat riders if it closes. Therefore, the bridge need not close.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Okada lives near the bridge.","Whenever a critic's relative would gain from a closing, the structure the critic wants closed need not close.","Boat taxis are slower than walking.","Some bridges should close.","Cousins sometimes agree with each other."],"a":1,"e":"The premises give a critic and a relative who would gain. The principle says that pattern is enough for \"need not close.\" The conclusion follows. The attractive choice — Okada lives near the bridge. — loses because an address does not connect the cousin's gain to \"need not close.\" The sweeping principle would make that step go through, which is what sufficiency requires here."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":3,"s":"A pastry chef says the bridge cables should be replaced. Therefore, the bridge cables should be replaced.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Cables on this bridge are made of steel.","Pastry chefs often bake before dawn.","The cables were installed more than twenty years ago.","Some engineers enjoy pastry.","Whatever this pastry chef says about bridge cables is correct."],"a":4,"e":"The chef recommends replacement. If that chef is correct about cables, the cables should be replaced. The other choices never use the recommendation. The attractive choice — Cables on this bridge are made of steel. — loses because steel is a fact about the cables, not a bridge from the chef's remark to a replacement decision. \"Whatever this chef says about cables is correct\" is what makes the remark sufficient."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"A sign-in clipboard reduced lost badges at the craft museum. The director concludes that the same rule will reduce lost badges at the science hall.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The science hall receives more visitors per day than the craft museum.","The craft museum began the rule on a holiday weekend.","At both museums, badges are lost at an unattended desk, and the desks are staffed in the same way.","The science hall uses numbered lockers for coats.","Clipboard paper at the craft museum was replaced every evening."],"a":2,"e":"The same kind of unattended desk is the likeness that lets the craft-museum result count in favor of the science hall. The attractive choice — The science hall receives more visitors per day than the craft museum. — loses because a larger crowd names a difference between the sites. Difference, by itself, does not show that the clipboard would touch the same kind of loss. The matching desks do."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"Dr. Feldman, author of the harbor-piling manual, says the elm pilings should be replaced this year. The board concludes that they should be replaced this year.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Feldman's remark applies that manual's measurements and replacement rules to the elm pilings as they stand this year.","Feldman is also a well-known speaker at boating festivals.","The manual was first published more than twenty years ago.","Several board members have not read the manual.","A newspaper printed Feldman's remark without comment."],"a":0,"e":"Applying the manual's own rules to this year's measurements makes the citation a reason about these pilings, not merely a name. The attractive choice — Feldman is also a well-known speaker at boating festivals. — loses because festival fame is more of the celebrity the argument already leans on. It does not show that this remark used the relevant rules on these pilings. The measurement choice does."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":2,"s":"The bridge carried every harvest float for a decade. The marshal concludes that it will carry this year's float.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The float is decorated with the same colors it has used for a decade.","The parade has used this bridge rather than the main road for the whole decade.","The bridge was painted last spring.","This year's float weighs less than every float the bridge has already carried.","Crowd estimates for the parade have been reliable."],"a":3,"e":"A float lighter than every successful crossing is inside the record the marshal is using. Colors and paint are not loads. The attractive choice — The float is decorated with the same colors it has used for a decade. — loses because matching colors feel like continuity. Color does not show that this float is a load the bridge has already survived. A lighter float does."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":3,"s":"The council dismisses Okada's call to close the bridge because Okada's cousin gains boat riders if it closes, and the council concludes the bridge need not close.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["A second engineer, with no boat business, also recommended closing the bridge.","Okada offered no measurement, and an inspection firm with no boat business found the beams sound.","Boat taxis have lost riders over the past year.","Some residents prefer the view from the bridge to the view from a boat.","Okada lives several miles from the bridge."],"a":1,"e":"Sound beams from an uninvolved firm support \"need not close\" on the merits. A second call to close is evidence on the other side. The attractive choice — A second engineer, with no boat business, also recommended closing the bridge. — loses because another engineer calling for a closing feels like important news about the bridge, and it uses the same dispute. It cuts against \"need not close.\" Sound beams from an uninvolved firm are what support the council's conclusion. A second call to close does the opposite."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":3,"s":"Most east-path maintainers want more gravel. The office concludes that most east-path users want more gravel.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The maintainers were surveyed on a rainy afternoon, when gravel washes out.","Gravel costs less than a boardwalk.","Most users live near the creek.","Maintainers own shovels.","On age, how they use the path, and opinions about gravel, the maintainers match the users."],"a":4,"e":"If the maintainers match the users on the question being asked, their majority is evidence about the users. Rain during the survey does not create that match. The attractive choice — The maintainers were surveyed on a rainy afternoon, when gravel washes out. — loses because a rainy survey feels relevant to gravel and actually threatens the sample: people standing in washouts are a special moment, not a match to users. The match on use and opinion is what strengthens the transfer."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Seed packets fell from half of nursery items sold to 30 percent. The buyer concludes that fewer seed packets were sold.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["A different clerk recorded the later period.","Seed packets cost less than flowering plants.","The nursery sold the same number of items in both periods.","Most packet buyers buy nothing else.","The nursery sells no items that are not either packets or plants."],"a":2,"e":"A fixed total and a share falling from one half to 30 percent means fewer packets. Who held the pencil does not settle the total. The attractive choice — A different clerk recorded the later period. — loses because a new clerk feels like a glitch in the comparison. It does not fix the total number of items, which is the fact that turns a smaller share into fewer packets. A recording change leaves the total free."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each pane in the stack is under the weight one clerk can lift. The builder concludes that the whole stack is under that weight.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The stack contains only one pane.","The clerk can lift a single pane onto a cart.","The panes are all the same shade of glass.","The greenhouse faces south.","Glass costs more than plastic sheet."],"a":0,"e":"If there is only one pane, the stack and the pane are the same object, so the weight limit carries. Being able to lift \"a\" pane was already given. The attractive choice — The clerk can lift a single pane onto a cart. — loses because lifting one pane restates the premise in the language of the conclusion. A stack of many panes can still exceed the limit. A one-pane stack cannot."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":2,"s":"The council dismisses Okada's call to close the bridge because a cousin gains boat riders, and concludes the bridge need not close.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Okada has twice urged closing a bridge that later proved sound.","Boat taxis have lost riders this year.","The cousin criticized the bridge at a public meeting.","An inspection firm with no boat business found cracked supports and recommended closing the bridge.","Okada lives several miles from the bridge."],"a":3,"e":"Independent cracks are a reason to close that does not rest on Okada's household. That undercuts \"need not close.\" The attractive choice — Okada has twice urged closing a bridge that later proved sound. — loses because past false alarms feel like a reason to wave Okada off, which is already the council's conclusion. They support \"need not close\" if they support anything. Cracked supports on this bridge cut against that conclusion."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":3,"s":"The bridge carried every harvest float for a decade. Therefore, it will carry this year's float.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The parade has used this bridge rather than the main road for the whole decade.","This year's float weighs far more than any float the bridge has carried.","The float is decorated with the same colors every year.","The bridge was painted last spring.","Crowd estimates for the parade have been reliable."],"a":1,"e":"A decade of lighter floats does not reach a float heavier than all of them. The weight breaks the likeness the prediction needs. The attractive choice — The parade has used this bridge rather than the main road for the whole decade. — loses because staying on the same bridge repeats the setting of the successful record. It feels like continuity and does not show that this float is a new kind of load. The extra weight does."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":3,"s":"Offices that added lunchtime walks later reported fewer fatigue complaints than offices that did not. The director concludes that the walks reduced fatigue.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Some offices without walks improved a little as well.","The walks take twenty minutes.","The director enjoys walking.","The walks happen outdoors when the weather allows.","The offices that added walks had also cut workloads in half, and the other offices had not."],"a":4,"e":"A workload cut confined to the walk offices is a rival cause of the fatigue gap. The walks are no longer the only difference on offer. The attractive choice — Some offices without walks improved a little as well. — loses because a small improvement elsewhere sounds like a challenge to the walks. It does not explain why the walk offices improved more. A workload cut only in those offices does."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"No archive inspection has reported silverfish. Therefore, the archive contains no silverfish.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The inspectors are not trained entomologists.","Silverfish are smaller than a grain of rice.","The inspections opened only the reading room, and silverfish in this archive live in the unopened map drawers.","The archive building is more than a century old.","No one on the staff likes silverfish."],"a":2,"e":"A search of the wrong room does not show the drawers are empty. The conclusion treats that search as if it had covered the archive. The attractive choice — The inspectors are not trained entomologists. — loses because a missing credential feels like a reason to distrust the search. It is weaker than a search that never looked where the insects would be, and a non-specialist can still record a visible insect. Unopened drawers show the silence is uninformative."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":2,"s":"The tide table is reliable because it matches the harbormaster, who is reliable because of the tide table. Therefore, the table's reliability has been established.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Neither the table nor the harbormaster has ever been checked against anything but the other.","The table and the harbormaster agree with each other.","The harbormaster is famous along the coast.","The table uses unusually clear type.","Many boaters own a copy of the table."],"a":0,"e":"If each source has been compared only with the other, their match is not an independent check. The claim that reliability has been established is worse off. The attractive choice — The table and the harbormaster agree with each other. — loses because agreement is the circle itself, restated as if it were new support. Learning they were never checked against anything else makes that circle the whole of the evidence, which undercuts \"established.\""},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":2,"s":"Every January rental is winterized. Cabin 7 is winterized. Therefore, cabin 7 is rented in January.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Cabin 7 is the only winterized cabin on the lake.","January rentals are inspected in December.","Cabins that are not winterized are rented in July.","Cabin 7 was winterized for its owner's private use and is not offered for rent in January.","Cabin 7 has a wood stove as well as insulation."],"a":3,"e":"Winterizing was necessary, not sufficient. A winterized cabin withheld from the January market shows the reversed conclusion is false. The attractive choice — Cabin 7 is the only winterized cabin on the lake. — loses because being the only winterized cabin seems to pin this cabin to the January rule. Uniqueness does not make it a January rental. Private use with no January offer is a winterized cabin that is not rented then, which is the counterexample."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":3,"s":"A celebrated pastry chef says the bridge cables should be replaced. Therefore, they should be replaced.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The chef has won international cooking awards.","Bridge engineers examined these cables and concluded they should stay in place.","The bridge is more than fifty years old.","The cables are made of steel.","The chef lives in the same city as the bridge."],"a":1,"e":"Engineers telling the city to leave these cables in place undercut a replacement conclusion that rested on a chef. The attractive choice — The chef has won international cooking awards. — loses because awards feel like a reason to trust the chef, which is the argument's own move. More awards do not answer an engineering finding that the cables should stay. That finding cuts against replacement."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Conditional reversal/negation","d":3,"s":"If a crate is stored in the hill warehouse, then it is logged in the red book. Crate K is not logged in the red book. Crate M is logged in the red book.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Crate M is stored in the hill warehouse.","Crate K is logged in some book other than the red book.","No crate logged in the red book is stored in the hill warehouse.","Crate M is not stored in the hill warehouse.","Crate K is not stored in the hill warehouse."],"a":4,"e":"Storage is sufficient for a red-book line, so a crate with no such line was not stored. That is K. M's line leaves M's location open. The attractive choice — Crate M is stored in the hill warehouse. — loses because a red-book line is necessary for hill-warehouse storage, not sufficient. M's line is what a stored crate would have. It does not show M was stored. K's missing line does show K was not stored."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":2,"s":"The oven stopped heating. A burned-out element, a tripped breaker, or a gas shutoff could each stop it. The notes do not say which of those occurred.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The element burned out.","The breaker did not trip.","The notes do not establish whether the element, the breaker, or the gas shutoff stopped the oven.","The oven stopped because of a gas shutoff.","None of the three causes could stop this oven."],"a":2,"e":"Each of the three could have stopped the oven, and the notes do not pick. So they do not establish which one did. They also do not clear any of the three. The attractive choice — The element burned out. — loses because the element is one of the three causes the notes leave open. Naming it feels like a finding. The notes' refusal to choose is the fact that follows, not a winner among the three."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":2,"s":"A remark that offers a claim as its only ground does not establish that claim. Oren's only ground for \"the bell is on time\" is that the bell is on time.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Oren's remark does not establish that the bell is on time.","Any remark about the bell is circular.","The bell is on time.","The bell is not on time.","Oren checked the bell against a second clock."],"a":0,"e":"Oren's only ground is the claim itself, and that pattern does not establish the claim. The bell's actual punctuality is untouched. The attractive choice — Any remark about the bell is circular. — loses because the principle covers a claim offered as its own ground. It does not reach every comment about the bell. The attractive choice widens one empty remark into a ban on the subject."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"A result in one setting supports the same result in a second setting only if the settings are alike in the way that matters. A phone ban reduced noise in a reading room. Nothing is said about whether ward noise has that same source.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["A phone ban will not reduce noise in the wards.","Ward noise is mostly from phones.","The reading room and the wards are alike in the way that matters.","The reading-room result does not, by itself, show that a phone ban will reduce noise in the wards.","The reading-room ban failed to reduce noise."],"a":3,"e":"The principle requires a relevant likeness before the result transfers, and none is given. So the reading-room result does not by itself show a quieter ward. The attractive choice — A phone ban will not reduce noise in the wards. — loses because without a likeness, the result does not transfer. That is not a proof that the ban will fail on the ward. The attractive choice upgrades a missing comparison into a prediction of failure."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Conditional chain","d":3,"s":"Every shuttle that stops at the pier collects a token. Every shuttle that collects a token is logged before dusk. The north shuttle was not logged before dusk. The south shuttle collected a token.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The south shuttle stopped at the pier.","The north shuttle did not stop at the pier.","The south shuttle was not logged before dusk.","The north shuttle collected a token.","No shuttle that collects a token stops at the pier."],"a":1,"e":"A pier stop would collect a token and then be logged. The north shuttle was not logged, so it did not stop at the pier. The south shuttle's token does not place it at the pier. The attractive choice — The south shuttle stopped at the pier. — loses because a token is necessary for a pier stop only in the sense that a pier stop produces a token. Collecting a token does not show the south shuttle stopped at the pier, because the rule does not say tokens come only from the pier. No log does show the north shuttle missed the pier."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":3,"s":"The kiln reached its target on all twelve firings this month, each with the same clay, load, and fuel. The next firing is planned with those same three settings. No one claims the kiln cannot fail.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The next firing must reach the target.","The next firing will miss the target.","The clay, rather than the fuel, caused every past success.","Fuel settings do not affect kiln temperature.","There is reason to expect the next firing, so planned, to reach the target, though that result is not guaranteed."],"a":4,"e":"The next firing copies the settings of twelve successes. That is a reason to expect another success. It is not a proof that the kiln must succeed, and it does not isolate clay as the cause. The attractive choice — The next firing must reach the target. — loses because twelve matched firings support an expectation. \"Must\" turns the expectation into a guarantee the record does not give. A kiln can still fail on the thirteenth try."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"Badges are lost at an unattended desk at both the craft museum and the science hall, the desks are staffed in the same way, and a sign-in clipboard cut those losses at the craft museum.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The science hall will have no lost badges at all.","The craft museum result is irrelevant to the science hall.","There is reason to expect a similar clipboard to cut that same kind of loss at the science hall.","The two halls differ in every way that matters to lost badges.","Clipboards cause visitors to lose badges."],"a":2,"e":"Same kind of desk, same staffing, and a clipboard that cut that loss once: that is a reason to expect a similar cut, not a promise of zero losses. The attractive choice — The science hall will have no lost badges at all. — loses because a matched setup supports expecting a similar cut. Zero losses is a stronger ending than the craft museum's result, which was a reduction, not a disappearance."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"The orchard will not use netting. It will use reflective tape, and that tape has kept similar crops from being ruined. No other threat to this crop is mentioned.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["Skipping netting does not, on these facts, show that the crop will be ruined.","The crop will be saved.","Netting is the only protection that has ever worked.","The orchard will use netting after all.","Birds are never a threat to fruit."],"a":0,"e":"A working alternative is already planned, so \"no nets, therefore ruin\" does not follow. Success for this crop is not itself guaranteed by a similar-crop record. The attractive choice — The crop will be saved. — loses because tape that has worked on similar crops blocks the leap from \"no nets\" to \"ruin.\" It does not prove this crop will be saved, because \"similar\" is not \"this crop is safe.\" \"Will be saved\" is one step past the facts."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":2,"s":"An inspection firm with no boat business found cracked supports and recommended closing the bridge. The council's only reply is that the engineer who first raised the alarm has a cousin who gains riders if the bridge closes.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The bridge is safe to leave open.","The cousin fabricated the cracks.","The inspection firm also gains boat riders.","The cousin's gain does not answer the inspection firm's finding.","No bridge with a cracked support should ever be repaired."],"a":3,"e":"A motive pointed at the first engineer leaves the firm's cracks untouched. So the gain is not an answer to the firm. It is not a finding that the bridge is safe. The attractive choice — The bridge is safe to leave open. — loses because the cousin's gain is aimed at a different person from the firm. It does not rebut the firm, and that is what the facts support. \"The bridge is safe\" contradicts the cracks rather than following from the reply."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":3,"s":"Maintainers and users of the east path match on age, on how they use the path, and on stated views about gravel. Most of the maintainers want more gravel.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["Every user wants more gravel.","There is reason to think most users want more gravel.","No user wants more gravel.","The maintainers were never asked.","Gravel is cheaper than wood, so users must want it."],"a":1,"e":"The groups match on the relevant points and most maintainers want gravel. That is a reason to think most users do. It is not a census of every user. The attractive choice — Every user wants more gravel. — loses because a matched majority supports \"most,\" not \"every.\" The attractive choice keeps the gravel conclusion and drops the limit from most of a matched group to all users."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":3,"s":"Honey sales and the new market rose in the same weeks. The clerk treats that overlap as proof that the market caused the rise. Overlap, by itself, is not proof of a cause.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Honey sales rose in the same weeks as the new market.","The market was new that season.","Markets always cause honey sales to rise.","The clerk works for the town.","The clerk has not proved that the market caused the rise in honey sales."],"a":4,"e":"The argument grants the overlap and denies that it proves causation. That denial is the conclusion. The attractive choice — Honey sales rose in the same weeks as the new market. — loses because the overlap is background. The argument's point is that the overlap fails as proof. A true premise is not the conclusion just because it is the most concrete sentence."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"Green badges of this kind go only to people who finish the dock course. Riley holds one. So Riley finished the dock course.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Green badges of this kind go only to people who finish the dock course.","Riley holds a green badge.","Riley finished the dock course.","Every person who finished the course holds a green badge.","Riley is an inspector."],"a":2,"e":"\"So\" marks the finish as the claim being drawn. The rule and the badge are why it is drawn. The converse, that every finisher holds a badge, is not drawn. The attractive choice — Green badges of this kind go only to people who finish the dock course. — loses because the badge rule is the premise that, with Riley's badge, yields the finish. The finish is the conclusion. The attractive choice is the rule, which is doing the supporting."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"The garage holds the same number of vehicles as last year, and bikes' share fell from 40 percent to 25 percent. Therefore the garage holds fewer bikes.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["The garage holds fewer bikes.","Bikes' share of the vehicles fell from 40 percent to 25 percent.","The garage holds the same number of vehicles as last year.","The garage holds no cars.","Forty percent is a larger share than 25 percent."],"a":0,"e":"\"Therefore\" marks the count of bikes. The share and the fixed total are the reasons, not the conclusion, and \"no cars\" goes beyond both. The attractive choice — Bikes' share of the vehicles fell from 40 percent to 25 percent. — loses because the share drop is a premise. With a fixed total it yields fewer bikes, and fewer bikes is the conclusion. The share is the more technical sentence, which makes it look like the point."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"This year's float is far heavier than any float the bridge has carried. A decade of lighter floats therefore does not show that the bridge will carry this year's float.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["This year's float is far heavier than earlier floats.","The bridge will collapse under this year's float.","The parade should be canceled.","A decade of lighter floats does not show that the bridge will carry this year's float.","The bridge carried ten years of floats, so it will carry this one."],"a":3,"e":"The argument stops at the decade's failure to show success with a heavier float. It does not establish collapse or a cancellation. The attractive choice — This year's float is far heavier than earlier floats. — loses because the extra weight is the premise. The conclusion is about what the decade fails to show. \"The bridge will collapse\" and \"cancel the parade\" are practical leaps the argument does not make."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":3,"s":"Okada's cousin gains boat riders if the bridge closes. Citing only that, the council dismisses Okada's warning and concludes the bridge need not close.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["citing an independent inspection that found the beams sound","rejecting a warning because of a relative's stake rather than because of evidence about the bridge","showing that netting is not the only way to save a crop","treating each light case as proof the set of cases is light","defining \"original\" in two ways"],"a":1,"e":"The warning is set aside for a family interest. That interest is the whole method. The attractive choice — citing an independent inspection that found the beams sound — loses because no inspection appears. The attractive choice is what a good version of this argument might have done. This argument cites the cousin and stops."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":3,"s":"Each map folds into a pocket, so the packet of maps folds into a pocket.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["treating a condition needed to fold a map as sufficient for being allowed on the trail","rejecting the hiker's advice because the hiker sells maps","projecting next weekend's weather from a dry month","turning a smaller percentage of maps into a smaller number without a total","attributing to a collection a feature established only for its members"],"a":4,"e":"Pocket-size is shown for the maps one by one and then assigned to the packet. That is parts to whole. The attractive choice — treating a condition needed to fold a map as sufficient for being allowed on the trail — loses because no trail rule appears. The attractive choice hears \"pocket\" and supplies a conditional flaw. The move is from each map to the packet."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Bikes' share of the garage fell, and the total number of vehicles is not discussed. The buyer concludes that the garage holds fewer bikes.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["attacking the buyer's motive for preferring cars","using \"bike\" in a mechanical sense and then in a legal sense","inferring a drop in number from a drop in share alone","concluding the fountain is quiet because each pump is, while equating the fountain with its loudest pump","citing last summer as the only ground for this summer"],"a":2,"e":"The share falls and the count is concluded, with no total. That is the percentage-to-number move. The attractive choice — attacking the buyer's motive for preferring cars — loses because no motive is mentioned. The attractive choice hears a buyer and invents an ad hominem. Share is being read as number."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Riley has the badge that finishing the course is sufficient to earn. Therefore, Riley finished the course.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["treating a sufficient condition for the badge as though it were a necessary one","polling only donors and concluding about all visitors","weighing this year's float against a decade of lighter ones","matching an outside gauge to a tide table","oiling a bell and then inferring a cause from the next day's lateness"],"a":0,"e":"The course is enough for the badge, and the badge is then treated as enough for the course. That reverses a sufficient condition. The attractive choice — polling only donors and concluding about all visitors — loses because there is no poll. The attractive choice is a sample flaw with none of the argument's people except a vague \"therefore.\" The move is a one-way badge rule run backward."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":2,"s":"**Okada's cousin gains boat riders if the bridge closes.** Citing only that, the council dismisses Okada's warning.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is an independent inspection result about the beams.","It is the conclusion that the bridge is safe.","It is a definition of \"cousin.\"","It is the sole ground offered for dismissing the warning.","It is a concession that the beams are cracked."],"a":3,"e":"The council cites that fact alone. It is support for the dismissal, not the conclusion that the bridge is safe and not an inspection. The attractive choice — It is an independent inspection result about the beams. — loses because the cousin's gain feels like a finding and is easy to promote into an inspection. No beams are measured in that sentence. It is the reason given for the dismissal, not a structural report."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":3,"s":"Each case is light enough to carry. **The full set is light enough for one person to carry.** That is what the quartet concludes.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the premise about each case.","It is the conclusion of the argument.","It is an objection by the streetcar conductor.","It is a definition of \"quartet.\"","It is evidence that one case is heavy."],"a":1,"e":"The quartet concludes the bold sentence. The cases one by one are the support. The attractive choice — It is the premise about each case. — loses because both sentences mention carrying, so the set's sentence is easy to file with the premise about the cases. The argument says the set's sentence is the conclusion. The premise is the sentence about each case."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":3,"s":"Bikes' share fell from 40 percent to 25 percent. **The garage holds fewer bikes.** The buyer concludes that from the share drop alone.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the premise about the percentages.","It is an objection offered by a mechanic.","It is a definition of \"garage.\"","It is evidence that the total number of vehicles doubled.","It is the conclusion."],"a":4,"e":"The buyer concludes the bold sentence from the share drop. The share drop is the premise. The attractive choice — It is the premise about the percentages. — loses because fewer bikes is the arithmetical-looking sentence, so it is easy to treat it as the percentage premise. The percentage premise is the earlier sentence. Fewer bikes is what the buyer concludes."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: a museum may sell a donated work only if the donor agreed to sales and the work is a duplicate. The Reed sketch was donated with no agreement about sales, and it is a duplicate. The Lau print is a duplicate and its donor agreed to sales.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The museum must sell the Lau print.","The museum may sell the Reed sketch.","The principle does not permit selling the Reed sketch.","The principle does not permit selling the Lau print.","The museum may sell every duplicate, including works whose donors refused sales."],"a":2,"e":"Selling the Reed sketch would violate a necessary condition. The principle therefore does not permit that sale. It does not order the Lau sale. The attractive choice — The museum must sell the Lau print. — loses because the Lau print meets both necessary conditions, so a sale is not forbidden. \"Only if\" does not create a duty to sell. \"Must sell\" turns a failed ban into an order. The Reed sketch lacks the donor's agreement, so a sale of it is not permitted."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: an endorsement establishes a cable replacement only if the endorser applies a relevant engineering standard to those cables. A pastry chef, applying no engineering standard, says these cables should be replaced. That remark is the only endorsement.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The remark does not establish that the cables should be replaced.","No engineer could establish a cable replacement either.","The cables should be replaced.","The chef applied an engineering standard.","The cables should be removed tomorrow morning."],"a":0,"e":"The only endorsement applies no relevant standard, so it does not establish replacement. The principle does not decide what an engineer could show. The attractive choice — No engineer could establish a cable replacement either. — loses because the principle withholds establishment from endorsements that skip the standard. It does not say engineers are powerless. The chef skipped the standard. An engineer who applied it is outside the principle's ban."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: silence in a log establishes absence only if any instance would have been logged and the log is complete. Every jam would have been logged, the log is complete, and no jam is logged.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The silence establishes nothing about jams.","There was a jam that the log missed.","The log is incomplete.","The silence establishes that there was no jam.","Motors never jam."],"a":3,"e":"Any jam would be in a complete log, and the log shows none. The principle says that silence establishes absence. The attractive choice — The silence establishes nothing about jams. — loses because \"silence establishes nothing\" is the right slogan when the principle's conditions fail. Here both conditions are met. The attractive choice applies the slogan and ignores the two conditions that switch it off."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":3,"s":"Principle: a claim offered as its own only ground is not adequate evidence for that claim. Oren's only ground for \"the bell is on time\" is that the bell is on time.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The bell is late.","Oren has not given adequate evidence that the bell is on time.","The bell is on time.","Oren checked a second clock.","Every statement about bells is circular."],"a":1,"e":"Oren's only ground is the claim itself, so the principle says the evidence is not adequate. The bell's actual time is not decided. The attractive choice — The bell is late. — loses because the principle grades the evidence, not the bell. \"Late\" is a factual verdict the evidence's emptiness does not deliver."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":3,"s":"Principle: if this year's float is heavier than any float the bridge has carried, a decade of success does not establish that the bridge will carry this year's float. This year's float is heavier than any of those floats.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The bridge will collapse.","The bridge will carry the float.","This year's float is lighter than the earlier ones.","Decades of success always transfer to heavier loads.","The decade does not establish that the bridge will carry this year's float."],"a":4,"e":"The float is heavier than the recorded loads, so the principle says the decade does not establish success with this float. The attractive choice — The bridge will collapse. — loses because the principle says the decade fails as proof. It does not say the bridge will fail. Collapse is the picture a heavier float suggests, and it is more than a failure of this evidence."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":2,"s":"The council cites only the cousin's gain and dismisses the warning. That citation does not show the bridge need not close, because a motive is not evidence about the beams.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["Any warning from someone with a relative in a related business is false.","Bridges should close whenever a cousin gains riders.","A motive for offering a warning does not, by itself, show that the warned-about step is unnecessary.","Family ties are the best evidence about beams.","Councils should ignore inspections."],"a":2,"e":"The argument limits what a motive can show. It does not declare every motivated warning false, and it does not praise family ties as evidence. The attractive choice — Any warning from someone with a relative in a related business is false. — loses because the argument does not say the warning is false. It says the motive, alone, does not show the closing is unnecessary. \"Any such warning is false\" is a harsher rule than the one used."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":2,"s":"Each case is light, and nothing is said about the combined weight. So the quartet has not shown that one person can carry the set.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["A feature of each member does not, by itself, establish that same feature for the collection.","Whatever is true of each member is true of the collection.","Light cases cannot be carried on a streetcar.","Quartets travel only by streetcar.","One heavy member makes every member heavy."],"a":0,"e":"The argument refuses to give the set a feature shown only for the cases. That refusal is the principle. The attractive choice — Whatever is true of each member is true of the collection. — loses because that principle would require the leap the argument blocks. It has the same parts-and-whole vocabulary and the opposite direction."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":2,"s":"Bikes' share fell, and the total number of vehicles is unknown. So it has not been shown that there are fewer bikes.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["A smaller share always means a smaller number.","Percentages are never about counts.","Garages never add vehicles.","A smaller share does not establish a smaller number when the total may have grown enough to offset it.","The person who counted the bikes also counted the cars."],"a":3,"e":"The argument withholds the count because the total is unknown and might offset the share. That is the principle. The attractive choice — A smaller share always means a smaller number. — loses because that is the arithmetic the argument says is unsafe while the total is open. Same percentages, opposite rule."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":3,"s":"This kind of badge is issued only to people who finish the dock course, and Riley holds one. So Riley finished the course.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["If a condition is sufficient for a result, then anyone who has the result met the condition.","If a result is produced only by a certain condition, then anyone who has the result met the condition.","Badges prove nothing about courses.","Inspectors receive every badge.","Courses are unnecessary for badges."],"a":1,"e":"The badge comes only from finishing, so holding it yields finishing. That is the \"only if\" principle, not the reversed sufficient-condition fallacy. The attractive choice — If a condition is sufficient for a result, then anyone who has the result met the condition. — loses because the argument uses \"only,\" which runs from the badge back to the course. The attractive principle runs a merely sufficient condition backward, which is the mistake this argument avoids. The wording is nearly the same and the logical direction is not."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":3,"s":"The clinic that added hand-washing stations recorded more infections the next month, not fewer.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The clinic added hand-washing stations.","Staff were told to wash their hands more often, and audits showed they did.","The clinic treated the same number of patients as in the previous month.","Hand-washing stations are recommended by several hospital manuals.","The new stations came with a test that detects mild infections the old test missed, and the underlying infection rate fell."],"a":4,"e":"If the new test counts cases the old test ignored, recorded infections can rise while true infections fall. That removes the conflict with the new stations. The attractive choice — The clinic added hand-washing stations. — loses because adding the stations is the setup that makes the rise surprising. A more sensitive test explains more recordings while the real rate falls. Restating the stations explains nothing."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"After the ferry added a dawn trip, it carried fewer passengers per week than before the trip was added.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["A dawn trip was placed on the schedule.","The dawn trip was advertised in the weekly rider bulletin.","The dawn trip replaced two busier midday trips rather than being added on top of them.","Fares were the same on the dawn trip as on the midday trips.","The ferry's seating capacity was not reduced."],"a":2,"e":"Replacing two busy trips with one dawn trip can shrink the weekly total even though a new departure exists. The attractive choice — A dawn trip was placed on the schedule. — loses because the new trip is the puzzle's setup. Losing two busy trips explains a weekly drop that a pure addition would not produce."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The town that added evening streetlights had more reported evening collisions that season than in the previous, darker season.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The new lights came with a rule that residents report every minor scrape, and minor scrapes had gone unreported before.","Streetlights were added before the reports rose.","Traffic volume on the lighted streets was about the same as in the darker season.","The lights met the brightness level the town's traffic office requested.","Police staffing on those streets did not increase."],"a":0,"e":"If minor scrapes suddenly have to be reported, the report count can rise even if the lights made serious crashes no more common. The attractive choice — Streetlights were added before the reports rose. — loses because the lights are the puzzling change. A new duty to report minor scrapes explains a rise in reports without a rise in serious crashes."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The garden that watered more often had drier soil at dawn than the garden that watered less.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["One garden was watered more often than the other.","The frequently watered garden received more gallons per week than the other garden.","Both gardens held the same plants in the same soil mix.","The frequent watering was done at noon onto covered beds that shed the water, while the other garden watered the soil itself at dusk.","Neither garden received rain that week."],"a":3,"e":"Water dumped on a cover at noon does not wet the soil. Dusk watering that hits the soil can leave it wetter at dawn despite fewer waterings. The attractive choice — One garden was watered more often than the other. — loses because the extra waterings are the puzzle. Water that never reached the soil explains a drier dawn reading. Repeating the schedule does not."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":3,"s":"Every cabin rented in January is winterized. Cabin 4 is rented in January. Therefore, cabin 4 is winterized.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["Every loaf on the morning rack was baked before dawn. This loaf was baked before dawn. Therefore, it is on the morning rack.","Every loaf on the morning rack was baked before dawn. This loaf is on the morning rack. Therefore, this loaf was baked before dawn.","This loaf was not baked before dawn. Therefore, it is not on the morning rack. Some dawn-baked loaves are not on the rack.","The rack was full last January. Therefore, it will be full next January.","Either the loaf is on the rack or it was baked after dawn. It is not on the rack. Therefore, it was baked after dawn."],"a":1,"e":"Both the stimulus and the right answer apply \"all A are B; this is A; therefore this is B.\" The dawn-then-rack choice is the converse error. The attractive choice — Every loaf on the morning rack was baked before dawn. This loaf was baked before dawn. Therefore, it is on the morning rack. — loses because the trap keeps the bakery words and reverses the rule: it affirms the necessary result and infers the sufficient condition. The stimulus affirms the sufficient condition, a January rental, and infers the necessary result, winterizing."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":3,"s":"Each pump is under the limit, and the fountain is only as loud as its loudest pump. Therefore, the fountain is under the limit.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["Each pane is under the lift limit. Therefore, a stack of ten panes is under the lift limit.","The stack is under the lift limit. Therefore, each pane is under it.","Panes are glass. Therefore, the greenhouse is glass.","The limit was set by the council. Therefore, the fountain is quiet.","Each pane is under the lift limit, and the stack contains only one pane. Therefore, the stack is under the lift limit."],"a":4,"e":"Both add a premise that stops the whole from outrunning the parts, and only then conclude about the whole. The attractive choice — Each pane is under the lift limit. Therefore, a stack of ten panes is under the lift limit. — loses because the trap drops the extra premise that keeps the whole from exceeding the parts. The stimulus keeps that premise, and so does the one-pane stack. Ten panes without that premise is the flawed version."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":2,"s":"It did not rain, the path drains quickly, it was underwater, and an open line is the only other way it floods. Therefore, the line was left open.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["The lamp is dark. Therefore, the bulb burned out.","The breaker tripped. Therefore, the lamp is dark.","The lamp was not unplugged, the bulb is new, the lamp is dark, and a tripped breaker is the only other way this lamp goes dark. Therefore, the breaker tripped.","It did not rain. Therefore, no path can be underwater.","Bulbs are glass. Therefore, breakers trip."],"a":2,"e":"Both eliminate every listed cause but one and then conclude that the remaining cause occurred. The attractive choice — The lamp is dark. Therefore, the bulb burned out. — loses because darkness alone picks a cause. The stimulus first removes the other listed causes. The breaker choice does that removal. The burned-out bulb does not."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":2,"s":"Reading-room noise was phone audio, and a ban reduced it. Ward noise is monitors, and phones there are already silent. Therefore, the reading-room result is a poor reason to expect quieter wards.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["Path puddles come from packed soil, and grit fixed them. Track puddles come from a missing drain, and grit does not add a drain. Therefore, the path result is a poor reason to expect a dry track.","Grit fixed the path. Therefore, grit will fix the track.","The track has no drain. Therefore, grit fixed the path.","Phone bans always quiet hospitals.","Monitors are a kind of phone, so the ban will work on the ward."],"a":0,"e":"Both identify a different cause in the second setting and conclude that the first result is a poor reason to expect the same result there. The attractive choice — Grit fixed the path. Therefore, grit will fix the track. — loses because the trap is the analogy the stimulus rejects. Same topics, opposite reasoning. The drain choice, like the ward choice, cites a different cause the remedy does not touch and refuses the transfer."},
{"t":"Parallel flaw","pattern":"Parallel flaw","d":2,"s":"Every guide who may lead night hikes has completed the radio check. Nia has completed the radio check. Therefore, Nia may lead night hikes.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["Every clerk who may issue a tide permit has passed the chart quiz. Luis has not passed the chart quiz. Therefore, Luis may not issue a tide permit.","If a clerk may issue a tide permit, then that clerk has passed the chart quiz. Luis may issue a tide permit. Therefore, Luis has passed the chart quiz.","Some people who pass the chart quiz are not clerks. Luis passed the quiz. Therefore, Luis is not a clerk.","Every clerk who may issue a tide permit has passed the chart quiz. Luis has passed the chart quiz. Therefore, Luis may issue a tide permit.","No one who failed the chart quiz may issue a tide permit. Luis failed the quiz. Therefore, Luis may not issue a permit."],"a":3,"e":"Both treat a necessary condition as sufficient: the check, or the quiz, is had, and the permission is inferred. The attractive choice — Every clerk who may issue a tide permit has passed the chart quiz. Luis has not passed the chart quiz. Therefore, Luis may not issue a tide permit. — loses because the trap is valid: it denies the necessary condition and denies the permission. The stimulus treats the necessary radio check as if it were sufficient for leading, and the Luis choice that infers the permit from the quiz does the same. Denying the quiz is not that flaw."},
{"t":"Parallel flaw","pattern":"Parallel flaw","d":3,"s":"The matinee audience liked the play. Therefore, nearly all theatergoers like the play.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["A sample that matches the store's customers in age and habits liked the tea. Therefore, there is reason to think the customers like the tea.","Shoppers already in the premium aisle liked the new tea. Therefore, all of the grocery's customers like the new tea.","The tea sold out. Therefore, it was displayed in the premium aisle.","Either customers like the tea or the shop will close. They do not like the tea. Therefore, the shop will close.","The buyer's cousin sells tea. Therefore, the sold-out report is false."],"a":1,"e":"Both treat an unmatched, special subgroup as the whole group. The matched sample does not. The attractive choice — A sample that matches the store's customers in age and habits liked the tea. Therefore, there is reason to think the customers like the tea. — loses because the trap includes a match between sample and group, which removes the stimulus's flaw. The premium-aisle choice, like the matinee, leaps from an unmatched subgroup. The matched sample is the repaired version."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":3,"s":"Offices that added walks later had fewer fatigue complaints. The director concludes that the walks caused the improvement.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the director enjoys walking","What color the offices paint their halls","Whether the word \"fatigue\" has more than two syllables","Whether the director's office has a window","Whether the walk offices also cut workloads, or otherwise changed the afternoons, while the other offices did not"],"a":4,"e":"The useful fact is one that would either hand the gap to a rival change or leave the walks as the remaining difference. The director's taste does neither. The attractive choice — Whether the director enjoys walking — loses because the director's hobby uses the walks and does not decide the causal claim. A yes leaves the complaints untouched, and a no does too. A workload cut only in the walk offices would weaken the walks, and the absence of any such cut would strengthen them."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"Bikes' share fell from 40 percent to 25 percent. The buyer concludes that the garage holds fewer bikes.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether 25 is less than 40","Whether bikes have two wheels","Whether the total number of vehicles rose enough to offset the smaller share","Whether the garage was repainted","Whether the buyer likes bikes"],"a":2,"e":"The count is sensitive to the total and insensitive to a restatement of the share. The useful question is about the total. The attractive choice — Whether 25 is less than 40 — loses because that 25 is less than 40 is already known and is the premise. Confirming it does not settle the count. The total's movement does: growth can raise the number, and a flat or smaller total lowers it."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"Finishing the dock course is enough to earn a green badge. Riley has a green badge. The clerk concludes that Riley finished the course.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether green badges are issued in any way other than finishing the course","Whether the course meets on weekdays","Whether badges hang on lanyards","Whether Riley likes docks","Whether the course was once longer than it is now"],"a":0,"e":"The reversed conditional stands or falls on whether the badge has another source. The calendar does not decide that. The attractive choice — Whether the course meets on weekdays — loses because the weekday schedule can be either way and Riley's badge still may or may not require the course. Another route to the badge would sink the clerk, and \"no other route\" would secure the clerk."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"The path was underwater. The groundskeeper concludes that the irrigation line was left open.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the groundskeeper has held the job for many years","Whether the line is painted green","Whether the groundskeeper prefers irrigation to watering by hand","Whether rain or another cause, with the valve closed, left the path underwater","Whether morning is before noon"],"a":3,"e":"The favored cause is insecure until the rivals are checked. Job tenure does not check them. The attractive choice — Whether the groundskeeper has held the job for many years — loses because tenure can be long or short without selecting among causes. Rain with a closed valve would weaken the conclusion, and a ruled-out alternative would strengthen it."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Pilar: The elm footbridge should be closed, because its beams are cracked. Rafael: The beams are cracked, but the bridge should stay open, because a posted weight limit would keep the heavy floats off it.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the beams are cracked","the elm footbridge should be closed","a weight limit can be posted on a bridge","floats can be heavy","Pilar and Rafael are discussing a bridge"],"a":1,"e":"Pilar says close it. Rafael says keep it open. The cracks are common ground, not the dispute. The attractive choice — the beams are cracked — loses because both speakers say the beams are cracked. The attractive choice is the most vivid shared fact. They differ on whether the bridge should close."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Esme: Riley finished the dock course, because Riley has the green badge and finishing is enough to earn one. Farid: Finishing is enough to earn the badge, but inspectors who never take the course also get badges, and Riley is an inspector, so the badge does not show that Riley finished.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["finishing the course is enough to earn a green badge","Riley has a green badge","there is a dock course","badges can be green","Riley's badge shows that Riley finished the dock course"],"a":4,"e":"Esme treats the badge as proof of finishing. Farid says the inspector route blocks that proof. The sufficient rule is agreed. The attractive choice — finishing the course is enough to earn a green badge — loses because both grant that finishing is sufficient. The attractive choice is that shared rule. They disagree on whether Riley's badge shows Riley finished, given the inspectors' route."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":2,"s":"Noor: The chef's remark establishes that the cables should be replaced. Omar: The remark is a pastry chef applying no cable standard, so it does not establish that.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["a pastry chef made a remark about the cables","cables are part of the bridge","the chef's remark establishes that the cables should be replaced","engineering standards can exist","Noor and Omar heard a remark"],"a":2,"e":"Noor says the remark establishes the decision. Omar says it does not. That is the issue. The attractive choice — a pastry chef made a remark about the cables — loses because both accept that the chef spoke. They disagree on whether the remark establishes replacement. The speech act is shared. Its force is not."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":2,"s":"Tessa: The full set is light enough for one person to carry, because each case is. Uri: Each case is light, but the four together exceed the limit, so the set is not light enough.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the full set is light enough for one person to carry","each case is light enough to carry","there are cases in the set","a streetcar can have a weight limit","Tessa and Uri counted four cases"],"a":0,"e":"Tessa says the set is light enough. Uri says it is not. The individual cases are common ground. The attractive choice — each case is light enough to carry — loses because both accept that each case is light. Uri uses that fact and then denies it of the set. The attractive choice is the agreed premise. The dispute is the set."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Post hoc","d":2,"s":"The chapel bell was oiled on Monday and rang late on Tuesday. The sexton concludes that the oiling caused the late ringing.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Oiling a bell always makes that bell ring late.","The sexton has oiled bells in other chapels.","Monday is the day the chapel polishes brass.","Nothing that happened between the oiling and the late ringing, other than the oiling, accounts for the lateness.","Late ringing is easier to hear than an on-time ring."],"a":3,"e":"If some other Monday-to-Tuesday change made the bell late, the oiling is not established as the cause. The conclusion depends on no such rival. The attractive choice — Oiling a bell always makes that bell ring late. — loses because a universal law would be more than this one inference needs. The sexton can be right about this bell even if some other oiled bell rings on time. What the inference needs is that no rival event explains this particular lateness. \"Always\" is a sufficient-looking slogan, not the necessary exclusion."}
]);
